#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "9efd33d6-c1d7-55ee-947e-0d067461d5c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-215" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = -6t-3t^2$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(-6t - 3t^{2}) \\\\\n&=-6 - 6t \\\\\n&=-6(1 + t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087386 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/id #uuid "136e4bd1-f9c4-50a0-9d16-54709f0d6d45" :question/math-academy-id "e-216" :question/problem "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t) = 1+2t+3t^2+4t^3$. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t=0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 + 2t + 3t^{2} + 4t^{3}) \\\\\n&= 2 + 6t + 12t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2 + 6t + 12t^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 + 24t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=0$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(0) &= 6 + 24(0) \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=0$ is $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular 